I did. And I'm going to share it with you for nothing.

4
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:20,080
But this is how to make a million dollars being a Scout leader.

5
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:24,980
Okay? You got a pencil? Paper? Are you listening?

6
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:30,500
This is how you're going to make a million dollars as a Scout leader.

7
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:35,060
First, get two million dollars.

8
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:38,140
And the rest is easy.

9
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:40,420
Yeah.

10
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:44,120
Hey, this is podcast number 30.

11
00:00:54,980 --> 00:01:15,100
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

12
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:16,560
This is Clark Green.

13
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:25,460
I'm recording this one a little ahead of time because this week I am up in Algonquin Provincial Park in Ontario, Canada,

14
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:28,340
with two crews of Scouts from our troop.

15
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:33,980
Doing a little paddling and portaging, or portaging, depending on how you were raised.

16
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:40,060
Yeah, it's been a hot summer here in the Mid-Atlantic and in the South.

17
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:43,120
And I guess just about everywhere. It's been kind of warm.

18
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:48,560
And it's highs in the 70s up there in Canada.

19
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:50,080
And I'm looking forward to it.

20
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:52,600
Yeah, it's getting down into the 50s at night.

21
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:53,460
Oh, baby.

22
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:54,400
Yeah.

23
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:57,380
Yeah, it's going to be a nice break from the heat.

24
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:02,740
But anyway, we've got several things to do on today's podcast.

25
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:08,220
In Scout Mastership, in seven minutes or less, we're going to continue talking about troop meetings.

26
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:10,640
And you know what?

27
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:11,580
You know what I found?

28
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:16,840
I have a fourth installment about the future of your unit and your future plans as a Scout Leader.

29
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:18,120
How about that?

30
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:19,580
You thought you were going to get away with three?

31
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:20,100
No.

32
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:21,100
Uh-uh.

33
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:22,720
I've got a fourth one for you.

34
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:24,900
Yep, it'll be in this podcast.

35
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:26,980
Plus a Scout Masters Minute.

36
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:31,080
Sounds like a lot to do, so we better get started, shall we?

37
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:34,380
Scout Mastership in seven minutes.

38
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:36,240
Or less.

39
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:47,020
This is from Baden-Powell's Outlook.

40
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:49,840
This is something that's not too hard to find online.

41
00:02:50,260 --> 00:02:53,160
You know, Google Baden-Powell's Outlook.

42
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:56,160
And this is from June 1910.

43
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,740
So this is quite early in the Scouting Movement.

44
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:29,720
He says this,

45
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:34,720
That's a great deal of your patrol leaders, and nine times out of ten, they will play up to your expectation.

46
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:41,420
But if you are going to always nurse them and not trust them to do things well, you'll never get them to do anything on their own initiative.

47
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:43,560
That's Baden-Powell.

48
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:46,620
Way back when, a hundred years ago.

49
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:52,620
At this week's troop meeting, I counted eight active assistant Scout Masters.

50
00:03:53,580 --> 00:03:59,540
Now, following Baden-Powell's advice, we often have comparatively little to do.

51
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:01,660
We don't do a lot of instructing.

52
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:10,900
I mean, maybe two or three times in the past year, we've had an adult from our pool of Scout Masters instructing the Scouts.

53
00:04:11,579 --> 00:04:14,019
And we do next to no program planning.

54
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:19,000
All of these things are in the firm grip of our youth leadership.

55
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,380
So what do we do?

56
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:24,660
What do we do at troop meetings?

57
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:28,680
Well, three assistants are working hard on recruiting.

58
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:33,360
One is following the progress of several Eagle projects and applications.

59
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:37,000
I was meeting with a visiting Weeblow's parent.

60
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:42,420
And the remainder were observing the proceedings of the troop meeting from a respectful distance.

61
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:44,000
And that's it.

62
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:45,760
That's really it.

63
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:47,920
There's not a lot for us to do.

64
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:54,260
Now, I know Scout Masters spend an inordinate amount of time worrying over their troop meetings.

65
00:04:54,420 --> 00:04:59,400
And they sometimes work on the entertainment quality of the meeting.

66
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:05,400
So far as I can tell, a lot of them are actually running the meetings.

67
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:07,000
And this is really a big no-no.

68
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,320
A troop meeting is not a television show hosted by the Scout Master.

69
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:12,020
Or a podcast.

70
00:05:13,180 --> 00:05:15,640
Where I get to talk and talk and talk.

71
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:16,100
No.

72
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:24,320
Here's some thoughts from an old Scout Masters handbook that I found on theinquiry.net.

73
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:27,060
And if you want to go there and check it out, it's great.

74
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:30,540
It's got lots of old school resources on scouting.

75
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:36,980
Inquiry.net.

76
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:38,780
That'll get you to the website.

77
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:42,859
Here's what this advice said.

78
00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:49,760
As the years have gone by, some Scout Masters have set troop meetings on too high a pedestal.

79
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:51,760
They have spoken of it reverently.

80
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:54,860
And they spent hours and hours in perfecting his programs.

81
00:05:54,860 --> 00:06:01,220
And have seemed to consider that the conducting of a troop meeting successfully once a week was the whole idea.

82
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:09,020
And the purpose of the Scout movement is running a fine, lively meeting on Friday nights or Tuesdays or Thursdays.

83
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:11,260
And their work was done then for the week.

84
00:06:11,260 --> 00:06:20,800
There never was a falser idol set up than this glorification of the weekly troop meeting to the exclusion of other forms of scouting.

85
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:27,960
We must keep clearly in mind at all times that the weekly troop meeting is but a means to an end.

86
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:29,580
It is not an end in itself.

87
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:33,980
Its business is not to be the scouting of your troop for the week.

88
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:38,280
Its business is to make scouting for a week.

89
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:39,800
To inspire it.

90
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:40,660
To pep it up.

91
00:06:40,780 --> 00:06:42,540
To give it purpose and activities.

92
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:47,680
To make it extend all through the week in each patrol's and each boy's life.

93
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:53,140
We can't accomplish much actual work in an hour and a half or two hours every week.

94
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:57,720
But we can use those hours to motivate every other hour of the same period.

95
00:06:58,620 --> 00:07:04,980
And by motivate we mean to stimulate activity by providing a motive to every boy and every patrol.

96
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:11,640
The troop meeting can have great value by simply bringing the boys together for a common experience.

97
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:17,900
It can make them feel they belong together as part of the whole big scouting brotherhood.

98
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:25,099
Well, I read that and I could see myself in a couple of the comments there.

99
00:07:26,159 --> 00:07:26,440
Yeah.

100
00:07:27,159 --> 00:07:29,340
Big, lively, fun troop meeting.

101
00:07:29,580 --> 00:07:32,000
Got to be rocking and rolling all the times.

102
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:36,679
Got to be, you know, something exciting and interesting and things like that.

103
00:07:36,739 --> 00:07:40,580
Well, that's not, that's really not what we're looking for.

104
00:07:41,219 --> 00:07:42,679
It's great if that happens.

105
00:07:42,680 --> 00:07:46,700
But hopefully it's the scouts that have made that happen.

106
00:07:47,660 --> 00:07:56,340
And we talked before about a matter of perspective as to what was good and what looked like it was orderly and useful for scouts.

107
00:07:57,100 --> 00:08:02,420
According to our lights as full-grown adult men.

108
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:08,860
And we've talked that scouts have a very different idea of that.

109
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:13,900
You know, what they consider to be fun and challenging, we might just pass by.

110
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:22,460
But if we have, as Baden-Powell said, put the full responsibility of what happens in a troop meeting on our youth leadership,

111
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,440
they're going to shine.

112
00:08:24,820 --> 00:08:28,420
They're going to rise to your expectations and surpass them.

113
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:31,620
As long as you look at it in the right perspective.

114
00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:39,140
So that's the heart of what a scoutmaster's role is at a troop meeting.

115
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:47,280
You know, there's a widely circulated little narrative called the One Minute Scoutmaster.

116
00:08:47,420 --> 00:08:50,460
And I think it was based on some management science thing.

117
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:52,800
We're going to talk about that in the next podcast.

118
00:08:52,800 --> 00:09:04,300
But basically, you know, one of the tests for a successful troop meeting with you as a scoutmaster is you sit in a chair and you wait.

119
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:06,660
The scouts begin the meeting.

120
00:09:07,199 --> 00:09:08,520
They conduct their meeting.

121
00:09:08,979 --> 00:09:12,199
They do whatever it is they're going to do, whatever they've planned.

122
00:09:12,200 --> 00:09:15,140
And then they look at you towards the end.

123
00:09:15,820 --> 00:09:18,420
And they say, now it's your turn for a minute.

124
00:09:20,820 --> 00:09:21,920
And you stand up.

125
00:09:22,060 --> 00:09:23,360
You give your scoutmaster's minute.

126
00:09:23,880 --> 00:09:24,860
Say goodnight, scouts.

127
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:26,740
And that's about it.

128
00:09:27,860 --> 00:09:33,020
Now, actually, you're not going to be sitting in the chair the whole time because you're going to have the odd scoutmaster's conference to do.

129
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:35,480
You're going to be talking with your fellow leaders.

130
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:41,080
You might be planning your part of an upcoming outing or something like that.

131
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:46,780
Just making sure that you have enough tents to sleep in for the leaders and all that type of thing.

132
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:53,720
But you're not actually engaged and involved in the ongoing program of that troop meeting.

133
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:57,040
That might be an unfamiliar picture to some of us.

134
00:09:58,060 --> 00:10:03,380
But I've been on both sides of the dividing line there.

135
00:10:03,380 --> 00:10:12,200
I've been actively engaged and involved in running troop meetings and presenting things for the scouts.

136
00:10:12,980 --> 00:10:18,500
And I've been on the other side where I'm watching what's going on.

137
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:23,940
And I'll have a couple of things to talk to my senior patrol leader about at the end of the meeting.

138
00:10:24,280 --> 00:10:25,240
Did this well?

139
00:10:26,020 --> 00:10:27,220
This didn't go too well.

140
00:10:27,460 --> 00:10:28,500
How are you going to fix that?

141
00:10:29,060 --> 00:10:30,500
What's your plans for next time?

142
00:10:31,540 --> 00:10:39,440
And the boys are so happy when they're actually running the show that they'll come back.

143
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:45,040
It's not a Nielsen rating kind of thing where they're going,

144
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:47,500
Well, the scoutmaster wasn't really on this week.

145
00:10:48,320 --> 00:10:49,700
Yeah, I didn't like that show.

146
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:50,900
Who wrote that one?

147
00:10:51,240 --> 00:10:52,600
That one wasn't very good.

148
00:10:53,340 --> 00:10:53,580
No.

149
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:55,040
They're not an audience.

150
00:10:55,220 --> 00:10:56,240
They are the participants.

151
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:59,100
They are the ones that make this happen.

152
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:01,560
And that's what we need to keep in our heads

153
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:05,660
as we're looking forward into the fall and the winter in our troop meetings.

154
00:11:05,660 --> 00:11:10,280
Well, we're going to wind this up in the next podcast.

155
00:11:10,839 --> 00:11:14,160
We're going to just finish up talking about some of the broad underlying concepts

156
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:17,760
about what adults do at a troop meeting.

157
00:11:35,660 --> 00:12:00,900
Well, this is the fourth in our series of talking about the future of your scout unit.

158
00:12:00,900 --> 00:12:07,060
And we're going to talk about money and finances this time by answering this question.

159
00:12:07,459 --> 00:12:12,480
How will we reach and or maintain financial stability within our scout unit?

160
00:12:14,319 --> 00:12:20,220
Years ago, I can remember a day where we had to turn to our treasurer

161
00:12:20,220 --> 00:12:25,860
and ask him to lend us several hundred dollars to float the troop through the next several months

162
00:12:25,860 --> 00:12:27,660
because we had no money.

163
00:12:27,660 --> 00:12:29,760
We didn't have an effective fundraiser.

164
00:12:31,439 --> 00:12:38,920
We were kind of nonplussed about charging people dues and things like that.

165
00:12:39,240 --> 00:12:41,000
It wasn't working very well.

166
00:12:41,420 --> 00:12:43,920
We needed to get on a much more stable financial footing.

167
00:12:44,579 --> 00:12:46,760
And over time, we were able to do that.

168
00:12:48,319 --> 00:12:53,280
Every troop, pack, and crew I know of raises money to support its program.

169
00:12:53,280 --> 00:13:00,420
And I'm not concerned here necessarily with the specifics of how money is raised and the rules of fundraising.

170
00:13:01,160 --> 00:13:07,380
Now, if you need to learn about that, find the unit money earning application.

171
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:08,959
It's easy to find online.

172
00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:11,020
It's a PDF file and I'll link to it on the blog.

173
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:14,640
It explains the official guidelines for unit fundraising.

174
00:13:15,439 --> 00:13:16,560
They're pretty simple.

175
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:22,439
Raising funds should have obtainable goals.

176
00:13:22,880 --> 00:13:25,400
It should be simple and minimally disruptive.

177
00:13:26,119 --> 00:13:28,219
And it should represent value for money.

178
00:13:29,260 --> 00:13:34,839
Fundraising isn't something that you set aside the program and you go off and you do the fundraising

179
00:13:34,839 --> 00:13:36,319
and then you come back to the program.

180
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:37,859
Do you understand what I'm saying?

181
00:13:38,060 --> 00:13:39,599
It should be part of your program.

182
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:45,320
There should be opportunities in any fundraisers for scouts to do the things that scouts do.

183
00:13:46,700 --> 00:13:51,400
You know, whether it's washing cars or serving meals or delivering goods or performing a service,

184
00:13:51,840 --> 00:13:54,100
it's a learning experience for scouts.

185
00:13:54,680 --> 00:13:57,660
And it's something that you should definitely take advantage of.

186
00:13:58,040 --> 00:14:02,960
And it should be an expectation that this is when we do fundraising and this is how it happens

187
00:14:02,960 --> 00:14:04,300
and it should be fairly simple.

188
00:14:04,300 --> 00:14:11,400
Now, that being said, I've been through a number of frustrating and difficult fundraisers in the past

189
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:14,680
until we found a decent one that makes things easy.

190
00:14:14,760 --> 00:14:15,560
And it's no big secret.

191
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:17,380
We have a spaghetti dinner twice a year.

192
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:19,140
One in the spring, one in the fall.

193
00:14:19,500 --> 00:14:23,219
And people actually look forward to participating in the spaghetti dinner.

194
00:14:23,579 --> 00:14:26,160
The only thing that our families have to do is sell tickets.

195
00:14:26,660 --> 00:14:30,520
We ask them to be responsible for X number of tickets per family.

196
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:32,000
And it's a very obtainable goal.

197
00:14:32,479 --> 00:14:33,599
And that's what I said before.

198
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:35,800
It's very important to have an obtainable goal.

199
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:38,060
And then there you go.

200
00:14:38,420 --> 00:14:43,220
Now, I know a lot of troops and packs and crews will have some kind of an awards program

201
00:14:43,220 --> 00:14:46,620
for guys who raise the most funds and things like that.

202
00:14:46,700 --> 00:14:49,820
We just haven't ever seen a need for it and we don't do it.

203
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:51,520
But, you know, there's another possibility.

204
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:59,320
And if you don't like whining and crying and carrying on and grousing and complaining,

205
00:14:59,320 --> 00:15:04,520
what I want you to do is take your podcast listening device and advance it by about 45 seconds.

206
00:15:04,520 --> 00:15:10,340
Because what follows is Clark's opinion and only my opinion.

207
00:15:10,340 --> 00:15:13,180
And I really don't want to hear about it because it's just my opinion.

208
00:15:13,300 --> 00:15:14,340
But I don't like the popcorn.

209
00:15:14,340 --> 00:15:16,980
I'm sorry.

210
00:15:16,980 --> 00:15:18,920
I don't like the popcorn.

211
00:15:18,920 --> 00:15:20,560
I've never tasted the popcorn.

212
00:15:20,900 --> 00:15:24,460
But I'm stuck on the value for money part of fundraising.

213
00:15:25,900 --> 00:15:29,660
Don't sell me $3 worth of popcorn for $15.

214
00:15:29,660 --> 00:15:34,760
You know, I don't like the Girl Scout cookies for the same reason.

215
00:15:36,319 --> 00:15:40,640
Yes, I am old and crotchety and a little mean.

216
00:15:41,579 --> 00:15:46,699
But we're talking about funds and fundraising and the popcorn is a huge thing.

217
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:48,540
And there you have it.

218
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:49,339
There's my comment.

219
00:15:50,260 --> 00:15:53,480
Don't order your life by my likes and dislikes.

220
00:15:53,900 --> 00:15:55,000
But there's my comment.

221
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:01,400
So if you were smart, you just turned your podcast thingy ahead 45 seconds.

222
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:02,760
And now we're back.

223
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:05,680
And you don't have to listen to the nasty, crotchety old man.

224
00:16:08,740 --> 00:16:14,000
Like the rest of your unit program, you know, a fundraiser needs to have heart.

225
00:16:14,720 --> 00:16:16,960
It needs to be something that people are going to be interested in.

226
00:16:17,220 --> 00:16:20,060
And everybody can get behind and support rather than, you know,

227
00:16:20,060 --> 00:16:23,980
that annual or biannual thing that everybody dreads.

228
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:30,780
Um, financial stability in your unit means having kind of a long view and maintaining a budget.

229
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:33,040
Do you have a unit budget?

230
00:16:33,540 --> 00:16:34,900
It's important to have one.

231
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:38,200
Are you nickel and diming all your leaders to death?

232
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:43,580
This happens, I think, more often within a Cub Scout pack.

233
00:16:43,580 --> 00:16:50,360
Where, you know, the den leader goes out and buys the box of pipe cleaners and the popsicle sticks and the glue and the glitter.

234
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:56,400
And somehow loses the receipt and it doesn't ever quite get back and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

235
00:16:56,460 --> 00:16:58,740
Well, it was only five or ten bucks and I don't mind and everything.

236
00:16:59,240 --> 00:17:02,240
And then over a year, they spent a couple hundred bucks on program things.

237
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:05,960
And, you know, that can get people set up in the wrong way.

238
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:13,260
So, uh, there needs to be a clear road to being able to play for program things that, uh, people go out and get.

239
00:17:13,319 --> 00:17:14,060
It needs to be simple.

240
00:17:14,180 --> 00:17:15,339
It doesn't need to take weeks.

241
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:18,920
And, um, it, there needs to be a budget around it.

242
00:17:19,460 --> 00:17:22,780
Uh, when I was a Cub Master, we had a budget for our pack.

243
00:17:22,780 --> 00:17:27,200
And we had a budget for our dens.

244
00:17:27,940 --> 00:17:35,500
And any den leader was on notice that they could spend, you know, X number of dollars every month on anything they wanted for their program.

245
00:17:35,620 --> 00:17:36,320
No problems.

246
00:17:36,500 --> 00:17:37,520
No questions asked.

247
00:17:37,860 --> 00:17:41,420
We just need to see a receipt for, uh, the treasurer.

248
00:17:41,740 --> 00:17:43,920
And it was very simple and it worked pretty well.

249
00:17:44,320 --> 00:17:45,899
But having a budget is important.

250
00:17:46,520 --> 00:17:49,780
Um, there should be program money available.

251
00:17:49,940 --> 00:17:51,500
There should be equipment money available.

252
00:17:51,500 --> 00:18:01,120
And having a little bit of an emergency fund socked away for some kind of unknown, uh, uh, emergency that would require a little financing.

253
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:02,460
That's a pretty good idea, too.

254
00:18:03,320 --> 00:18:09,300
Uh, so, you know, a budget for a unit is not too much unlike a budget for a person.

255
00:18:10,380 --> 00:18:14,200
And, um, it's a good idea to have one and to stick to it.

256
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:18,800
Now, it surprises people to know that our little scout troop of 30 or 35

257
00:18:18,800 --> 00:18:23,600
has an overall annual budget of about $15,000 to $20,000.

258
00:18:24,480 --> 00:18:27,399
That's including everything from soup to nuts.

259
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:35,620
The food that we spend, that we get for camping weekends, you know, summer camp, our trip to Canada, uh, and, and everything like that.

260
00:18:35,899 --> 00:18:38,260
It's, it's, it's a powerful amount of money.

261
00:18:38,379 --> 00:18:39,879
Actually, I might be low-balling it there.

262
00:18:39,880 --> 00:18:43,460
It might be $25,000 to $30,000 that runs through our account in a given year.

263
00:18:43,960 --> 00:18:45,360
That's a serious amount of money.

264
00:18:46,080 --> 00:18:50,300
And that takes a good treasurer, and it takes a committee with their eyes open,

265
00:18:50,500 --> 00:18:54,340
and it takes good thorough reporting and good budgeting.

266
00:18:54,660 --> 00:18:55,860
I am not a money guy.

267
00:18:56,260 --> 00:18:57,480
I don't do good with money.

268
00:18:57,760 --> 00:18:59,940
I don't good, I'm not a good record keeper.

269
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:02,620
Um, I'm not a good budget maker.

270
00:19:02,620 --> 00:19:09,199
Uh, and, and knowing that is nine-tenths of the battle because I entrust that to other leaders in the troop

271
00:19:09,199 --> 00:19:11,179
and let them, let them take care of it.

272
00:19:11,340 --> 00:19:13,199
I, I just know that it's important.

273
00:19:14,780 --> 00:19:18,739
And what we're talking about is broad concepts, and we're talking about the next five years,

274
00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:25,260
you know, the future of your unit, maintaining financial stability, having good financial plans,

275
00:19:25,439 --> 00:19:28,979
having a fundraiser that everybody enjoys and doesn't dread.

276
00:19:28,980 --> 00:19:34,100
These are all important aspects to, uh, looking towards the future of your unit

277
00:19:34,100 --> 00:19:36,680
and maintaining its service in your community.

278
00:19:36,960 --> 00:19:41,400
So the next time we're together, we're going to talk about the final question in this series,

279
00:19:41,620 --> 00:19:46,720
which is, do we meet key indicators of effectively presenting our program?

280
00:19:51,120 --> 00:19:55,100
That's right, it's time for a Scoutmaster's Minute.

281
00:19:55,100 --> 00:19:58,320
That's right, it's time for my hands.

282
00:19:58,639 --> 00:19:59,719
Do, do, do, do.

283
00:19:59,820 --> 00:20:04,080
Biddy-bar, biddy-bar, biddy-bar, biddy-bar, biddy-bar, biddy-bar.

284
00:20:04,520 --> 00:20:08,340
One of the founders of the Boy Scouts of America, Ernest Thompson Seton,

285
00:20:08,899 --> 00:20:12,179
was visiting a camp he founded near his New Jersey home.

286
00:20:12,879 --> 00:20:18,159
And Seton had invited several important local businessmen to join him on this particular visit

287
00:20:18,159 --> 00:20:20,360
to interest them in supporting the camp.

288
00:20:20,360 --> 00:20:25,459
During their stay, they watched with great interest as some of the scouts there,

289
00:20:26,040 --> 00:20:27,439
they probably weren't scouts right then,

290
00:20:27,520 --> 00:20:30,520
because I think this was during the period where Seton was working, uh,

291
00:20:30,820 --> 00:20:33,520
on his woodcraft Indians idea.

292
00:20:33,740 --> 00:20:36,320
So this was probably before the founding of the BSA.

293
00:20:37,159 --> 00:20:40,260
But during their stay, they watched with great interest

294
00:20:40,260 --> 00:20:44,340
as some of the boys tried to light a fire by friction

295
00:20:44,340 --> 00:20:46,600
using the ancient bow and drill method.

296
00:20:46,600 --> 00:20:52,800
Their efforts were great, and the resulting fire was a victory won over many, many attempts.

297
00:20:53,480 --> 00:20:56,639
As they turned from the scene, one of the guests turned to Seton and said,

298
00:20:56,740 --> 00:20:59,439
Mr. Seton, why in a world of matches

299
00:20:59,439 --> 00:21:03,300
do you ask the boys to struggle with these primitive methods?

300
00:21:04,840 --> 00:21:06,520
Well, Seton thought about it for a moment,

301
00:21:07,159 --> 00:21:08,580
and he pointed at the ground.

302
00:21:09,240 --> 00:21:11,780
You are thinking of the fire that is lit here.

303
00:21:11,780 --> 00:21:15,820
And then he placed his hand over his heart and continued,

304
00:21:16,200 --> 00:21:20,520
while I am after the fire that is kindled here in the heart.

305
00:21:21,980 --> 00:21:26,080
The challenges that life present often turn us from an easy path

306
00:21:26,080 --> 00:21:29,220
to one that requires much more effort and more skill.

307
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:34,520
As scouts, we deliberately challenge ourselves to develop our skill as outdoorsmen

308
00:21:34,520 --> 00:21:36,820
and extend our own personal limits.

309
00:21:36,820 --> 00:21:40,240
There are a lot of easier ways to do things,

310
00:21:40,860 --> 00:21:44,220
but the way that offers growth is often the most difficult,

311
00:21:45,300 --> 00:21:47,460
and in the end, the most meaningful.

312
